WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.010
- [Narrator] Colorado and Maine have ruled

00:00:02.010 --> 00:00:04.500
that Donald Trump does
not qualify for the ballot

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.080
because of his involvement in January 6th.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.840
While these states have
either dismissed similar cases

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.640
or still have them pending,

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:14.970
now Trump has asked the
Supreme Court to weigh in.

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:16.830
The case hinges on this part

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:19.657
of the 14th Amendment,
Section 3, that says,

00:00:19.657 --> 00:00:21.870
"No person shall hold any office

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:24.000
who having previously taken an oath,

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.150
have engaged in
insurrection or rebellion."

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:29.010
It seems pretty straightforward,

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:32.310
but scholars say this language
raises a lot of questions.

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:34.860
- Does that apply to the
presidents of the United States?

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:37.650
What does it mean to have
engaged in insurrection

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:40.920
or rebellion against the United States,

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:43.500
and how do you determine whether

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:45.210
someone has done that or not?

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:47.220
- [Narrator] Here's the
history behind this amendment,

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.140
how it's been used in the past,

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:51.890
and what questions it could
bring to the Supreme Court.

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:56.820
- The 14th Amendment is the
most important amendment

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:58.500
ever added to the Constitution,

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.330
or certainly since the Bill of Rights.

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:01.920
You might say that it's an attempt

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.690
to put into the Constitution
some of the major results

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:07.980
of the Civil War.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:09.090
- [Narrator] This is Eric Foner.

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:10.920
He's written a lot on the 14th Amendment

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:12.960
and the time period in
which it was created.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:13.793
He is-

00:01:13.793 --> 00:01:15.750
- The sort of specialist on the Civil War

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:17.430
and Reconstruction period.

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:18.930
- [Narrator] One of his
books was even cited

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:21.000
in the Colorado Supreme Court's ruling.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.710
- My publisher was ecstatic.

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:25.560
They really think it's gonna
lead to a lot of sales,

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:27.750
which I'm not so sure about.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:29.820
- [Narrator] And in that
book, he details the origins

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:31.260
of the 14th Amendment,

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:33.450
the one that established
birthright citizenship,

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:35.010
equal protection under the law,

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:37.500
how representatives are apportioned,

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:41.550
oddly, the debt ceiling,
but also this, Section 3,

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:44.640
barring Confederates who
served from serving again.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.470
- Most Northern Republicans,

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:49.890
the victorious people in the Civil War,

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:53.550
thought that the 14th Amendment
was absolutely essential

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:56.790
to making sure that the
victory in the Civil War

00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:59.820
of the nation was consolidated

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:01.740
and that the former Confederates

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:04.020
didn't come into power again.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:07.410
I think white Southerners saw
Section 3 as a punishment.

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:08.970
- [Narrator] And while these sections

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:10.800
were fiercely debated in Congress

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:11.970
and throughout the country-

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:14.460
- [Eric] There's almost
no debate about Section 3.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:16.980
In fact, the debate about Section 3 was,

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:21.030
should the penalty be that
they could not hold office

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:24.000
or should the penalty be that these folks

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.920
could not have the right to vote?

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:26.850
- [Narrator] They eventually agreed

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:29.130
on just barring from office.

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:30.480
At first, it was formally invoked

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:33.090
just a few times to remove Confederates.

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:35.070
- Subsequently in American history,

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:39.420
on occasion it was enforced,
but very, very rarely.

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:41.400
- [Narrator] Most notably
is Victor L. Berger,

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:44.460
a congressman and co-founder
of the Socialist Party.

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:48.420
He was elected in 1918 after
being indicted for espionage.

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:51.180
- Now by the way, the Espionage
Act, it's important to say,

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:53.940
had nothing to do with espionage
the way we understand it.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:56.670
It was just if you oppose the war,

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:58.020
they threw you in jail.

00:02:58.020 --> 00:02:59.340
- [Narrator] Berger did oppose the war,

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:02.820
so Congress invoked Section
3 and refused to seat him.

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:03.930
The Supreme Court went on

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:06.360
to overturn his espionage conviction,

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:08.070
and then he was seated in Congress

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:10.560
and eventually served two more terms.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:12.240
- They said, "Well, if there's no evidence

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:14.040
that he did give aid or comfort

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:16.770
to the enemies of the United States now,

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:20.280
so therefore we can't deny
him the right to hold office."

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:21.900
- [Narrator] So that
raises the first questions

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:22.980
for the Supreme Court.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:25.710
What qualifies as an insurrection today?

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:27.720
- How do we know someone did that?

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:30.420
Are they gonna get a trial by jury?

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:33.300
Is it just an administrative
thing where some committee,

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:35.910
well, look, here's the
film and you're in there.

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:37.530
Is that a judicial proceeding?

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:39.330
Is that a political proceeding?

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:42.060
- [Narrator] And does it
require a criminal conviction?

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:43.350
Trump is charged with a lot

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:45.030
of things in the January 6th case,

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:46.920
but insurrection isn't one of them,

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:48.930
and the trial hasn't even begun.

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:51.690
One person involved in
January 6th has been unseated

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:53.190
due to Section 3.

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:54.900
A New Mexico County Commissioner,

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:56.970
so an officer who took an oath,

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:59.610
was convicted of
trespassing on January 6th

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:01.568
and served time in jail.

00:04:01.568 --> 00:04:03.497
- It's a great day for America.

00:04:03.497 --> 00:04:06.420
- [Narrator] He was removed
from office under Section 3,

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:08.190
but the judge specifically mentioned

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:10.020
that he would've been removed regardless

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:12.363
of whether he'd been
convicted of any crime.

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:14.670
Another important question,

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:17.490
does Section 3 even
apply to the president?

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:19.920
- Senator, Representative in Congress,

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:22.983
elector, for president,
for vice president,

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:29.150
or civil or military office
under the United States

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:31.020
or under any state.

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:36.020
Is the president an officer
under the United States?

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:37.560
Doesn't mention the president,

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:40.050
but it doesn't exclude
the president either.

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:42.930
- [Narrator] Another
question, who enforces this?

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:45.120
In these past examples,
it was either enforced

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:49.140
by the judiciary or Congress
decided to not seat someone,

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:51.420
but can it be enforced on a ballot?

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:54.330
- This is not barring you from running.

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:56.370
It is barring you from winning,

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:58.770
I guess, from holding office.

00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:01.470
There's not a jurisprudence about this.

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:02.430
- [Narrator] And trying to judge

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:04.320
the modern-day interpretation

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:07.620
of the original intent of
the amendment is tricky.

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:11.700
- If you are searching for
the one original intent

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:13.590
or the one original meaning,

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:15.330
you are never gonna get anywhere,

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:17.646
because there was so much dissension,

00:05:17.646 --> 00:05:21.480
there were disagreement,
there were compromises.

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:23.616
- [Narrator] So the lack of
specificity in the amendment

00:05:23.616 --> 00:05:26.910
and the scarcity of case
law since it was ratified

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:28.980
means there's a lot of
ways the Supreme Court

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:30.420
could interpret this law

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:33.290
and a lot of questions for them to answer.

00:05:33.290 --> 00:05:35.707
(soft music)

